   "subject": "Using external peaklist",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, <div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve been playing around with the Cardinal MSI package a little and would like to try some of the data analysis features on some older data without re-doing all the peaklists etc. Is there a way to simply coerce an R data.frame into a peaks class in Cardinal? For instance, my data frame has columns of Sample Name, X, Y, Peaks[1...n]. This would save me some time.</div><div><br></div><div>Heath Patterson</div><div>Chaurand Group</div><div>University of Montreal</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "mCull83y-ZYJ",
   "from": "kbemis",
   "author": "5276ff4387de2618",
   "date": "2015-02-17T17:48:45Z",
   "subject": "Re: Using external peaklist",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Heath,<div><br></div><div>Thank you for using Cardinal!</div><div><br></div><div>Does the code below reproduce a similar data.frame as yours?</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sample</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;A&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;B&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> each</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">9</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> expand</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">grid</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> peak1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">18</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> peak2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">18</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> peak3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">18</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>If so, then you should be able to do (for example):</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">msset </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">MSImageSet</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> coord</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;x&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;y&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;sample&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)])</span></div></code></div><br></div><div>where the &#39;spectra&#39; argument needs to be a matrix with each column as a mass spectrum / peaks for a single pixel.<br></div><div><br></div><div><b>Please note that the &#39;sample&#39; column <i>must</i> be named &#39;sample&#39; (in lower case), and it should probably be left out if you only one sample.</b></div><div><br></div><div>If you have a column for the m/z values, add an argument &#39;mz&#39; and set it to that column, too, or Cardinal will simply automatically generate mz=1,2,3...</div><div><br></div><div>To make sure Cardinal recognizes the dataset has peaks instead of a full spectra, do:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">centroided</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">msset</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE<br></span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>If you have multiple samples, remember to consider that when plotting images:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><div>image(msset, mz=1, subset=sample %in% &quot;A&quot;) # plot sample &#39;A&#39;</div><div>image(msset, mz=1, layout=c(1,2)) # plot both samples</div></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Let us know if your data.frames are different or if this doesn&#39;t work for you.</div><br>Thank you,</div><div>Kyle</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
